From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] DEBUG: ath11k: nss: rx release ring matching the firmware RBM (E83)

E83. Measured on IPQ5018 with the ring probe (journal 2026-08-22): in
NSS mode the host deliberately does not seed the RXDMA BUF ring

	/* RXDMA BUF ring is offloaded to NSS */
	if (!ab->nss.enabled)
		ath11k_dp_rxdma_ring_buf_setup(ar, rx_ring, HAL_RXDMA_BUF);

so the wifili firmware fills it with its own buffers - and it tags every
one of them with RET_BUF_MGR = 3 = HAL_RX_BUF_RBM_SW0_BM. Hardware
therefore returns those buffers to WBM2SW0, which this driver allocates
as tx_ring[0].tcl_comp_ring, and the probe indeed shows RXDMA releases
piling up there (hp 8 -> 72, tp never moves).

The ring the init message declares as the firmware's rx release ring is
dp->rx_rel_ring, i.e. WBM2SW3 (DP_RX_RELEASE_RING_NUM is a hardcoded 3),
and that ring stays all zeroes for the whole run - nothing ever reaches
it. So the firmware returns RX buffers to SW0 while being told its
release ring is SW3.

QSDK derives the RBM from the configured release ring id
(dp_rx_get_rx_bm_id -> HAL_RX_BUF_RBM_SW_BM(wbm_sw0_bm_id, rx_rel_id)),
so the measured RBM of 3 means the firmware works with release ring id 0.

Point the init message at the WBM2SW0 ring so it matches. Keep it behind
a module parameter, since the same ring is also tx_comp_ring[0] here and
the pairing is exactly what has to be proven:

	nss_rx_rel_wbm0=1  ring the firmware's RBM points at (default)
	nss_rx_rel_wbm0=0  previous behaviour, WBM2SW3

Not for submission - this is a measurement.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -23,6 +23,15 @@
 module_param(nss_init_stage, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(nss_init_stage, "wifili bring-up stage limit (debug bisection)");
 
+/* DEBUG E83: which WBM2SW ring is handed to the firmware as its RX release
+ * ring. 1 = WBM2SW0, the ring the firmware's own RET_BUF_MGR points at;
+ * 0 = dp->rx_rel_ring (WBM2SW3), what this driver has always sent.
+ */
+static unsigned int nss_rx_rel_wbm0 = 1;
+module_param(nss_rx_rel_wbm0, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(nss_rx_rel_wbm0,
+		 "hand WBM2SW0 to wifili as the rx release ring (debug)");
+
 
 /* DEBUG E78: on demand dump of the DP ring state - the rings live in host
  * DMA memory and the HP/TP registers are readable through ab->mem, so a
@@ -3323,9 +3332,22 @@
 	ath11k_nss_fill_srng_info(ab, dp->reo_reinject_ring.ring_id,
 				  &wim->reo_reinject_ring);
 
-	/* fill reo release ring info */
-	ath11k_nss_fill_srng_info(ab, dp->rx_rel_ring.ring_id,
-				  &wim->rx_rel_ring);
+	/* fill reo release ring info - E83: the firmware tags its own RX
+	 * buffers with RET_BUF_MGR = SW0_BM, so the hardware returns them to
+	 * WBM2SW0 (tx_ring[0].tcl_comp_ring here), not to dp->rx_rel_ring
+	 * (WBM2SW3). Hand it the ring its RBM actually points at.
+	 */
+	if (nss_rx_rel_wbm0)
+		ath11k_nss_fill_srng_info(ab,
+					  dp->tx_ring[0].tcl_comp_ring.ring_id,
+					  &wim->rx_rel_ring);
+	else
+		ath11k_nss_fill_srng_info(ab, dp->rx_rel_ring.ring_id,
+					  &wim->rx_rel_ring);
+
+	pr_info("nss-rxrel: rx_rel_ring id=%d (wbm0=%u, default rx_rel id=%d)\n",
+		wim->rx_rel_ring.ring_id, nss_rx_rel_wbm0,
+		dp->rx_rel_ring.ring_id);
 
 	/* fill reo exception ring info */
 	ath11k_nss_fill_srng_info(ab, dp->reo_except_ring.ring_id,
